<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<domain-models xmlns="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models
    http://axelor.com/xml/ns/domain-models/domain-models_7.4.xsd">

  <module name="aiaccountant" package="com.axelor.apps.aiaccountant.db"/>

  <!-- One bubble of the visible AI Accountant chat, exactly as the panel renders it.

    WHY IT EXISTS. A transcript kept only in the browser's localStorage, under a key containing the
    per-tab conversationId, vanishes whenever that key changes or that storage is cleared — after a
    reload that mints a new conversationId, in a new tab, in another browser, after a bundle
    upgrade. AiTurnMemory.historyJson holds the words too, but under a conversationId nobody can
    reach any more. This table is the durable transcript: written on the server, read by the
    server, and the only thing the panel displays.

    SCOPE IS (user, company) — NOT the conversation. This is the deliberate difference from
    AiTurnMemory, AiChatSession and AiWorkflowState, and the reason a second table exists rather
    than a column on one of those. Those three are keyed by conversationId because the Azure chain
    and, far more importantly, an authorised job MUST NOT cross from one browser tab to another: a
    job authorised by a sentence typed in one tab may not be continued by «продължи» typed in
    another. None of that reasoning applies to what the user is reading. A transcript is not an
    authorisation and grants nothing — it is one person's chat about one set of books, and it has to
    survive the very thing that invalidates a chain: a new tab, a new session, a new id. So the
    conversation isolation above stays as it is, and the transcript is keyed one level
    wider, by the two things that actually decide who may see these words.

    conversationId is still stored, but only so a row can be correlated with the chain that produced
    it when reading logs. NOTHING reads the transcript by conversationId, and it is not part of any
    key.

    ORDER AND DUPLICATES. `seq` is allocated per (user, company) and carries a unique constraint, so
    the order the user saw is the order that comes back, and two tabs appending at the same instant
    collide in the database rather than silently interleaving. `turnKey` is minted by the client per
    send: a retried or replayed request finds its row already there and changes nothing, which is
    what makes a refresh or a retry incapable of showing a message twice.

    WHAT IS NOT HERE. No credentials, no tokens, no tool arguments, no record payloads, no prompt
    or routing internals — only the text the panel displays and the tool-name list already shown
    beneath an assistant bubble. See AiUsageStat for telemetry, which is separate and is the only
    thing AiAccountantRetentionService prunes.

    LIFETIME. Until the user presses "Delete conversation", and only then. No scheduler, no startup
    task and no age bound removes a row here — deliberately, and AiAccountantRetentionServiceTest
    pins it. -->
  <entity name="AiChatMessage">

    <!-- `column` is explicit because `user` is an SQL keyword and the generator refuses to derive a
      column of that name. Same as AiTurnMemory, AiWorkflowState and AiChatSession. -->
    <many-to-one name="user" ref="com.axelor.auth.db.User" title="User" required="true"
      index="true" column="user_id"/>
    <many-to-one name="company" ref="com.axelor.apps.base.db.Company" title="Company"
      required="true" index="true"/>

    <!-- Position in the transcript, allocated per (user, company), oldest first. The ordering
      authority: createdOn is a timestamp and two rows written in the same millisecond would not be
      separable by it, which is precisely the case a chat produces. -->
    <long name="seq" title="Sequence" required="true"/>

    <!-- Which bubble this is: 1 user, 2 assistant, 3 error — the same three the panel renders, and
      no others. See AiChatTranscriptStore.Role, which is the only thing that writes it. No
      selection is declared because this entity has no form and no grid: it is read by the chat
      endpoint and by nothing else. -->
    <integer name="roleSelect" title="Role" required="true"/>

    <!-- What was said, verbatim, as the panel shows it. -->
    <string name="content" title="Content" large="true"/>

    <!-- The tool names already displayed under an assistant bubble, as a JSON array of strings.
      Display metadata only: no arguments, no results, nothing the user is not already shown. -->
    <string name="toolsJson" title="Tools"/>

    <!-- The client's own id for the send this row belongs to. Together with roleSelect it makes an
      append idempotent, so a retried POST or a replayed request cannot produce a second bubble. -->
    <string name="turnKey" title="Turn key" index="true"/>

    <!-- The chain this row was produced under. Correlation only — see the note above. -->
    <string name="conversationId" title="Conversation" index="true"/>

    <!-- Order integrity, enforced by the database rather than by the store's arithmetic: two
      concurrent appends cannot both take the same position. -->
    <unique-constraint columns="user,company,seq"/>
    <!-- Idempotency, likewise. A replay of the same send is refused here, not deduplicated later. -->
    <unique-constraint columns="user,company,turnKey,roleSelect"/>

  </entity>

</domain-models>
